tried to unmount a filesystem, but it failed. What could be the reasons,
and how would you troubleshoot?"

if a filesystem cannot be unmounted, the most common reason is that it's
still in use. I first check for open files or processes with lsof +D
`<mountpoint>`{=html} or fuser -vm `<mountpoint>`{=html}. I also verify
that no shell has its current working directory inside the mount using
pwd, and check for applications like databases, Docker containers, or
web servers using the filesystem. If it's an NFS mount, I consider stale
network connections. After safely stopping or terminating the processes,
I retry umount. If necessary, I may use umount -l for a lazy unmount or
umount -f for certain network filesystems, understanding the risks
before doing so.

Quick Cheat Sheet Command Purpose lsof +D /mnt/data List processes using
the mount fuser -vm /mnt/data Show PIDs accessing the filesystem pwd
Check if your shell is inside the mount w / who Check other logged-in
users swapon --show Check if swap is using the filesystem losetup -a
Check for loop devices umount -l /mnt/data Lazy unmount umount -f
/mnt/data Force unmount (mainly network filesystems)

These are the commands and reasoning an interviewer typically expects
from an experienced Linux or DevOps engineer.

