A Linux server is taking much longer than usual to boot. How would you
troubleshoot it?

If a Linux server takes longer than expected to boot, I first determine
whether the delay is in the kernel or userspace using systemd-analyze.
Then I identify slow services with systemd-analyze blame and inspect the
dependency chain using systemd-analyze critical-chain. I check for
failed services with systemctl --failed and review boot logs using
journalctl -b. I also investigate filesystem checks, disk health, full
filesystems, network or DNS timeouts, NFS mounts, and resource
bottlenecks such as CPU, memory, or I/O. Once I identify the root cause,
I fix the configuration or failing component and confirm the improvement
by measuring boot time again.

