A user is trying to create a file under a partition, but gets Permission
denied. What could be the reasons, and how would you troubleshoot?"

f I get a "Permission denied" error while creating a file, I first
verify the directory permissions with ls -ld and confirm the current
user using whoami and id. Next, I check ownership and whether the
filesystem is mounted read-only using mount. I also verify available
disk space with df -h and inode usage with df -i. If permissions appear
correct, I inspect ACLs using getfacl, and on RHEL systems I check
SELinux or on Ubuntu I check AppArmor. Finally, I review kernel logs for
filesystem errors. Once I identify the root cause, I apply the
appropriate fix---such as changing ownership, adjusting permissions,
remounting the filesystem read-write, freeing disk space, or correcting
security policies.

Quick Troubleshooting Commands Command What it checks ls -ld /path
Directory permissions and ownership whoami Current user id User ID and
group memberships mount \| grep /path Read-only (ro) vs read-write (rw)
mount df -h Disk space df -i Inode usage getfacl /path Access Control
Lists lsattr /path Immutable attribute namei -l /path Permissions on
each directory in the path getenforce / aa-status SELinux or AppArmor
status dmesg or journalctl -k Filesystem or kernel errors Senior-Level
Tip

In an interview, avoid saying "I'll just run chmod 777." That's
considered poor practice and a security risk.

