    execute present; S: execute missing SetGID s S s: execute present;
    S: execute missing Sticky Bit t T t: execute present; T: execute
    missing Interview Questions & Answers Q1. How do you set the Sticky
    Bit? chmod +t directory

or

chmod 1777 directory Q2. What is the purpose of the Sticky Bit?

It prevents users from deleting or renaming files owned by other users
in a shared directory. Only the file owner, directory owner, or root can
perform those actions.

Q3. What is the difference between s and S? s → SetUID/SetGID is enabled
and execute permission exists. S → SetUID/SetGID is enabled but execute
permission is missing. Q4. What is the difference between t and T? t →
Sticky Bit is enabled and execute permission exists. T → Sticky Bit is
enabled but execute permission is missing. Numeric Values of Special
Permissions Special Permission Numeric Value SetUID 4 SetGID 2 Sticky
Bit 1

